This is a short not how to write the code do that libltdl can get the
ptr to a function already in the inla-binary build. gcc/mingw-gcc only

Prepare the function you want to use by adding attributes, so that the
optimizer does not remove it. 


// Force the compiler to keep this symbol even with aggressive LTO enabled
__attribute__((used)) __attribute__((visibility("default")))
#if defined(__cplusplus)
extern "C"
#endif
double sin_intern(double x) 
{
	double ans = sin(x);
	printf("call sin_intern(%f) = %f\n", x, ans);
	return ans;
}

For linux/mac, we have to add linker option '-rdynamic'

For windows, its more tricky.  First include this header

#if defined(_WIN32)
#       include <windows.h>
#endif

Then we need a code-fix like below. The _WIN32 spesific code fix `a
known issue', which AI describes as

``This Win32 fallback code is required because libltdl has a
well-known architectural limitation on Windows when trying to look up
symbols inside the main executable itself (lt_dlopen(NULL)). While
libltdl works perfectly on Linux when passing NULL to open the main
program, its Windows implementation frequently fails to find valid,
exported internal functions like sin_intern.''

		lt_dlinit();
		handle = lt_dlopen(NULL);
		fun = (fun_tp *) lt_dlsym(handle, "sin_intern");
#if defined(_WIN32)
		if (!fun) {
			HMODULE hModule = GetModuleHandle(NULL);
			if (hModule) {
				fun = (fun_tp *) ((void *) GetProcAddress(hModule, "sin_intern"));
			}
		}
#endif
		const char *error = NULL;
		if (!fun && (error = lt_dlerror()) != NULL) {
			fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", error);
			exit(1);
		}

For windows, we also need to add linker option '-Wl,--export-all-symbols'

